<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/13th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Siege</p><h1 class="enc-title">Siege of Baghdad (1258)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1258">1258</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Mongol destruction of Baghdad, Mongol invasion of 1258, Mongol sack of Baghdad, sack of Baghdad</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> in 1258 was the capture and sack of the Abbasid capital by the Mongol army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hulagu-khan">Hulagu</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-mustasim">al-Musta'sim</a> was executed and much of the city's population killed. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate</a> treat the event as the end of the caliphate in Baghdad, and accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayyubid-empire">Ayyubid Empire</a> note its impact on the region.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/siege-of-baghdad-1258.jpg" alt="Siege of Baghdad (1258)" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sayf al-vâhidî et al. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bagdad1258.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> was founded in 762 by al-Mansur, the second Abbasid caliph, and became the centre of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islamic-golden-age">Islamic Golden Age</a>. From the tenth century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasids</a> lost power, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buyid-dynasty">Buyids</a> occupied Baghdad in 945 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuks</a> in 1055. From 1230 Mongol generals raided <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> almost every year, though Baghdad remained unconquered.</p><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongke">Möngke Khan</a> became khan in 1251, he sent his brother Hulegu west to destroy the Nizari Ismaili Assassins and secure the submission of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphs</a>, with one fifth of the empire's manpower. The Assassins' Grand Master surrendered on 19 November 1256. Hulegu had expected Caliph <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-mustasim">al-Musta'sim</a> to provide troops, and the caliph's ministers persuaded him to refuse. A correspondence followed in September 1257, in which the caliph's reply called Hulegu young and ignorant, and his envoys were mocked by mobs in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Hulegu's armies invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> from several sides. On 16 January 1258 the Mongol vanguard under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baiju">Baiju</a> broke the dykes of the Dujayl Canal and flooded the camp of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a>'s army, which was then routed. Hulegu reached the eastern suburbs on 22 January and encircled the city with a palisade and a moat. The assault began on 29 or 30 January, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catapult">catapults</a>, naphtha and arrows carrying guarantees of safety for those who had not fought. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> held the eastern wall by 4 February.</p><p>Hulegu insisted on unconditional surrender. On 7 February a large number of unarmed soldiers and inhabitants left the city and were divided into groups and executed. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> surrendered on 10 February with his family and 3,000 dignitaries. The sack began on 13 February and lasted a week. Hulegu spared Christians, scholars, sayyids and merchants who traded with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a>. On 20 February al-Musta'sim and his family were put to death; the caliph was wrapped in a carpet and trampled by horses, as the Mongols avoided shedding royal blood.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The number of dead is unknown. Later Muslim writers gave 800,000 to two million, and Hulegu himself claimed two million, a figure described as obviously inflated. An epidemic among the survivors raised the toll, and scholars have debated whether it was plague. Hulegu left on 8 March, ordering <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> rebuilt.</p><p>The fall ended the 500-year-old <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>. A member of the dynasty reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a> and was installed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluks</a> as al-Mustansir II, but he and his descendants were puppets of the Mamluk state. Power moved towards cities such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabriz">Tabriz</a>, capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilkhanate">Ilkhanate</a> that Hulegu founded. Under the Ilkhanate Baghdad prospered, unlike the exaggerations of later Muslim historians, though it declined relative to Tabriz. The fall has traditionally been seen as the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islamic-golden-age">Islamic Golden Age</a>, though its real consequences are uncertain.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Siege of Baghdad” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Even after the <b>Mongol destruction of Baghdad</b> in 1258, the shadow caliphate maintained by the Mamluks in Cairo (1261–1517) gave legitimacy to Muslim rulers across North Africa and the Levant.</blockquote><a href="/empire/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The ruler was at first an emir, but Abu Zakariya’s son Muhammad I al-Mustansir (r. 1249–1277) took the caliphal title of commander of the faithful in 1253, and after the <b>Mongol sack of Baghdad</b> in 1258 he was briefly recognised as caliph by the Sharif of Mecca.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hafsid-sultanate">Hafsid Sultanate · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Mongol sack of Baghdad</b> in 1258, in which the last caliph of Baghdad was put to death, ended that revival.</blockquote><a href="/essays/the-collapse-problem">The Collapse Problem</a></li></ul></section>
